STATIC NESTED CLASS

Interview Questions

1. What is a static nested class?
-->A static nested class is a class declared with the static keyword inside another class. It does not need an object of the outer 
    class to be created.

      Syntax:
      
      class Outer {
          static class Inner {
          }
      }




2. Difference between static nested class and member inner class?
-->
| Static Nested Class                     | Member Inner Class                            |
| --------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Declared with `static`                  | Declared without `static`                     |
| Doesn't need outer class object         | Needs outer class object                      |
| Can access only static members directly | Can access both static and non-static members |
| More memory efficient                   | Less memory efficient                         |





3. Can a static nested class access this of the outer class?
-->No.

      A static nested class has no reference to the outer class object, so it cannot use Outer.this.




4. How do you create an instance of a static nested class?
-->class Outer {
        static class Inner {
        }
    }
    
    public class Main {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Outer.Inner obj = new Outer.Inner();
        }
    }




5. Can a static nested class have static methods?
-->Yes.

      Since it is a static class, it can contain static methods and static variables.
      
      class Outer {
          static class Inner {
              static void display() {
                  System.out.println("Hello");
              }
          }
      }



6. Why do we prefer a static nested class?
-->Saves memory.
    Doesn't require an outer class object.
    Used when the nested class doesn't need to access outer object members.
    Makes code more organized.




7. Can a static nested class access outer class private variables?
-->Yes, but only static private variables directly.

      To access non-static private variables, it needs an outer class object.
      
      class Outer {
          private static int x = 10;
      
          static class Inner {
              void show() {
                  System.out.println(x);
              }
          }
      }
